# The corpus is a GENERATED artifact whose bytes are compared, not merely parsed.
#
# `check:corpus` regenerates it and asserts the result is byte-identical to the committed
# file. With `core.autocrlf=true` — the default on many Windows setups, and set on the
# maintainer's machine — git checks text files out as CRLF while the generator emits LF, so
# the gate would fail on Windows immediately after any fresh clone and pass on the Linux
# runner. That is a gate that reports the platform rather than the drift.
#
# Pinning LF here fixes the artifact's bytes on every platform. Deliberately scoped to the
# corpus alone: normalising the whole repository would rewrite every checked-out file for a
# problem only the byte-compared artifact has.
corpus/*.json text eol=lf
